findings.body:
This always sets `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` to the request `origin` (or `*`) together with `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`, and drops the allowlist.

**Failure mode:** a page at `https://evil.example` can make credentialed cross-origin calls to this API and read the responses, because the browser will accept the reflected ACAO. That exposes any cookie-authenticated or credentialed endpoint to cross-site data theft / CSRF-with-read.

**Also broken for the stated null-origin case:** `ACAO: *` + `ACAC: true` is rejected by browsers, so native clients that omit `Origin` still do not get a usable CORS result.

Restore an explicit allowlist (include known mobile webview origins if needed). Only set `ACAC: true` when the origin matches. Never pair `*` with credentials:

```suggestion
  if (origin && ALLOWED.has(origin)) {
    return {
      "Access-Control-Allow-Origin": origin,
      "Access-Control-Allow-Credentials": "true",
      Vary: "Origin",
    };
  }
  return {};
```

(Re-introduce `const ALLOWED = new Set([...])` with the real app and webview origins.)
